Z||000000||DTH||11-10-2001||09:35 AM||||I have a 1984 Fiero with a 2.5 and auto trans. I was wondering what V6 engine I could put in my car without major mods. I know with the V8 swap there would be tranny upgrade. I would like not to have tranny work done if it's possible. Also what about the wiring harness.  Thanks in advance.   Dan||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||11-10-2001||12:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DTH:<BR><B>I have a 1984 Fiero with a 2.5 and auto trans. I was wondering what V6 engine I could put in my car without major mods. I know with the V8 swap there would be tranny upgrade. I would like not to have tranny work done if it's possible. Also what about the wiring harness.  Thanks in advance.   Dan</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The 84 didn't have a V6 in its plans, so wiring can be a challenge. As for V6 engines, any 2.8 V6 Fiero engine would be the easist, however most 2.8 Fiero engines are high milage variety so REBUILD comes to mind.  The V6 could probably take your 84 transmission, but again high milage transmission REBUILD comes to mind. Other optional engines are 3.1L or 3.4L GM engines are the same block as the 2.8 so the install would be as difficult as a 2.8 swap and the engine is newer.<P>Unless you have done major engine swap work, IMHO, and if you want it done right have someone who has done these swaps before do it.  Just bite the bullet, cause when you do it yourself and get lost in the swap, recovery is not likely.  Too many things can go wrong, or other things need to be changed to have the swap done properly.  It would be $1-2 K just to replace the 2.5 L in the car.  Double it and you'd have a V6.<P>All depends on what you want the car for, how much time you have, the skill sets you have, the place to do the work and the biggest how deep is your wallet.<P>||||reg||1
